<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.6.2: Choosing the Best Method to Solve an Equation</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02426</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.6.2: Choosing the Best Method to Solve an Equation</md:title>
    <md:uuid>310002fb-a2ae-4f1c-ae5e-2c23dd0c91fd</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Write down your answer for how to solve each equation. Find the two solutions by rewriting it in factored form or by <term data-schema-version="“1.0”" class="glossary-tooltip">completing the square</term>. Be prepared to explain your choice of method.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Activity</title><exercise id="_0f5f4561-b47a-455e-8062-8d0aba4397b0" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> solutions for \(x^2−2x−1.25=0\).</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 2.5, -0.5</para><para>The correct answers are: 2.5, -0.5.</para></solution></exercise></section><exercise>
  <problem> 
    <para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>Completing the square method:</para>
  
  <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl} x^{2}-2 x-1.25 &amp;=&amp;0 \\
    x^{2}-2 x &amp;=&amp;1.25 \\
    x^{2}-2 x+\left(\frac{-2}{2}\right)^{2} &amp;=&amp;1.25+\left(\frac{-2}{2}\right)^{2} \\
    x^{2}-2 x+1 &amp;=&amp;1.25+1 \\
    x^{2}-2 x+1 &amp;=&amp;2.25 \\
    (x-1)^{2} &amp;=&amp;2.25 \\
    \sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} &amp;=&amp;\sqrt{2.25} \\
    x-1 &amp;=&amp;\pm \sqrt{2.25} \\
    x &amp;=&amp;1 \pm \sqrt{2.25} \\
    x &amp;=&amp;1 \pm 1.5 \\
    x &amp;=&amp;1+1.5 \\
    x &amp;=&amp;1-1.5 \\
    x &amp;=&amp;2.5 \\
    x &amp;=&amp;-0.5 \\ x&amp;=&amp;2.5 \text { and } x=-0.5
    \end{array}\)</para>

</solution></exercise>

<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="_60245351-60e4-44c6-982f-dba2c5c5710a" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> solutions for \(5x^2+9x−44=0\).</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 2.2, -4</para><para>The correct answers are: 2.2, -4.</para></solution></exercise></section><exercise>
  <problem> 
    <para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>Completing the square method:</para>
  
  <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl} 5 x^{2}+9 x-44 &amp;=&amp;0 \\
    5 x^{2}+9 x &amp;=&amp;44 \\
    \frac{5 x^{2}+9 x}{5} &amp;=&amp;\frac{44}{5} \\
    x^{2}+\frac{9}{5} x+\left(\frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{9}{5}\right)^{2} &amp;=&amp;\frac{44}{5}+\left(\frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{9}{5}\right)^{2} \\
    x^{2}+\frac{9}{5} x+\frac{81}{100} &amp;=&amp;\frac{44}{5}+\frac{81}{100} \\
    x^{2}+\frac{9}{5} x+\frac{81}{100} &amp;=&amp;\frac{961}{100} \\
    \left(x+\frac{9}{10}\right)^{2} &amp;=&amp;\frac{961}{100} \\
    \sqrt{\left(x+\frac{9}{10}\right)^{2}} &amp;=&amp;\sqrt{\frac{961}{100}} \\
    x+\frac{9}{10} &amp;=&amp;\pm \frac{31}{10} \\
    x &amp;=&amp;-\frac{9}{10} \pm \frac{31}{10} \\
    x &amp;=&amp;-\frac{9}{10}+\frac{31}{10} \\
    x &amp;=&amp;-\frac{9}{10}-\frac{31}{10} \\
    x &amp;=&amp;\frac{22}{10}=2.2\\
    x&amp;=&amp;-\frac{40}{10}=-4 \\
    x&amp;=&amp;2.2 \text { and } x=-4
    \end{array}\)</para>

</solution></exercise>

<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="e9061560-631d-4849-8168-d06ba9152699" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> solutions for \(x^2+1.25x=0.375\).</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 0.25, -1.5</para><para>The correct answers are: 0.25, -1.5.</para></solution></exercise></section><exercise>
  <problem> 
    <para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>Completing the square method:</para>
  
  <para>\(\begin{aligned} x^{2}+1.25 x &amp;=0.375 \\ x^{2}+1.25 x+((0.5)(1.25))^{2} &amp;=0.375+((0.5)(1.25)){ }^{2} \\ x^{2}+1.25 x+0.390625 &amp;=0.375+0.390625 \\ (x+0.625)^{2} &amp;=0.765625 \\ \sqrt{(x+0.625)^{2}} &amp;=\sqrt{0.765625} \\ x+0.625 &amp;=\pm 0.875 \\ x &amp;=-0.625 \pm 0.875 \\ x &amp;=-0.625+0.875 \\ x &amp;=-0.625-0.875 \\ x &amp;=0.25 \\ x &amp;=-1.5 \\ x=0.25 &amp; \text { and } x=-1.5 \end{aligned}\)</para>

</solution></exercise>

<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="f681bad2-7daa-463f-b9d9-36a177312aca" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> solutions for \(4x^2−28x+29=0\).</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(\frac{7}{2}+ \sqrt {5}\), \(\frac{7}{2}- \sqrt {5}\)</para><para>The correct answers are: \(\frac{7}{2}+ \sqrt {5}\), \(\frac{7}{2}- \sqrt {5}\).</para></solution></exercise></section><exercise>
  <problem> 
    <para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>Completing the square method:</para>
  
  <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl} 4 x^{2}-28 x+29 &amp;=&amp;0 \\
    \frac{4 x^{2}-28 x+29}{4} &amp;=&amp;0 \\
    x^{2}-7 x+\frac{29}{4} &amp;=&amp;0 \\
    x^{2}-7 x &amp;=&amp;-\frac{29}{4} \\
    x^{2}-7 x+\left(\frac{7}{2}\right)^{2} &amp;=&amp;-\frac{29}{4}+\left(\frac{7}{2}\right)^{2} \\
    x^{2}-7 x+\frac{49}{4} &amp;=&amp;\frac{20}{4} \\
    x^{2}-7 x+\frac{49}{4} &amp;=&amp;5 \\
    \left(x-\frac{7}{2}\right)^{2} &amp;=&amp;5 \\ \sqrt{\left(x-\frac{7}{2}\right)^{2}} &amp;=&amp;\sqrt{5} \\
    x-\frac{7}{2} &amp;=&amp;\pm \sqrt{5} \\ x&amp;=&amp;\frac{7}{2}\pm\sqrt{5} \\ x&amp;=&amp;\frac{7}{2}+\sqrt{5} \\ x&amp;=&amp;\frac{7}{2}-\sqrt{5} \\
    x&amp;=&amp;\frac{7}{2}+\sqrt{5} \text { and } x=\frac{7}{2}-\sqrt{5}
    \end{array}
    \)</para>

</solution></exercise>
</section>

<note class="self-check">
  <para>Use completing the square to solve \(2x^2+10x+8=0\).</para>
 </note>

 <section><title>Solve by Using Perfect Squares and Completing the Square </title>
  <para>Solve \(2x^2+5x+2=0\) by <term data-schema-version="“1.0”" class="glossary-tooltip">completing the square</term>.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Divide all terms by the leading coefficient. 
    \(\frac{2x^2+5x+2}{2}=\frac{0}{2}\) 
    \(x^2+\frac{5}{2}x+1=0\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Move the constant to the right side of the equation. 
    \(x^2+\frac{5}{2}x=-1\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Complete the square and add to each side: \((\frac{5}{2} \times \frac{1}{2})^2=\frac{25}{16}\). 
    \(x^2+\frac{5}{2}x+\frac{25}{16}=-1+\frac{25}{16}\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Simplify.  
    (Hint: create a common denominator before adding.) 
    \(x^2+\frac{5}{2}x+\frac{25}{16}=-\frac{16}{16}+\frac{25}{16}\)
    \((x+\frac{5}{4})^2=\frac{9}{16}\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Take the square root of each side. 
    \(\sqrt{(x+\frac{5}{4})^2}= \sqrt{\frac{9}{16}}\) 
    \(x+\frac{5}{4}= \pm \frac{3}{4}\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Write two equations and solve. 
    \(x+\frac{5}{4}=\frac{3}{4}\)         \(x+\frac{5}{4}=-\frac{3}{4}\)
    \(x=-\frac{2}{4}=-\frac{1}{2}\)   \(x=-\frac{8}{4}=-2\)</para>
  <note class="try look-at-me"/>
  <title>Try It: Solve by Using Perfect Squares and Completing the Square</title>
  <exercise>
    <problem>
      
      <para>Solve \(4x^2+36x+37=0\).</para>
    
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
    
    </problem><solution>
    <para>Here is how to solve using completing the square:</para>
    
    <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Divide all terms by the leading coefficient. 
      \(\frac{4x^2+36x+37}{4}=\frac{0}{4}\) 
      \(x^2+9x+\frac{37}{4}=0\)</para>
    
    <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Move the constant to the right side of the equation. 
      \(x^2+9x=-\frac{37}{4}\)</para>
    
    <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Complete the square and add to each side: \((9 \times \frac{1}{2})^2=\frac{81}{4}\). 
      \(x^2+9x+\frac{81}{4}=-\frac{37}{4}+\frac{81}{4}\)</para>
    
    <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Simplify.  
      \((x+\frac{9}{2})^2=11\)</para>
    
    <para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Take the square root of each side. 
      \(\sqrt{(x+\frac{9}{2})^2}=\sqrt{11}\) 
      \((x+\frac{9}{2})= \pm \sqrt{11}\)</para>
    
    <para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Write two equations and solve. 
      \(x+\frac{9}{2}=\sqrt{11}\)       \(x+\frac{9}{2}=-\sqrt{11}\)
      \(x=-\frac{9}{2}+\sqrt{11}\)       \(x=-\frac{9}{2}-\sqrt{11}\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
  </section>
